{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And the servants of Yitzchak dug in the valley, and they found there a well of living water.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, explained that the reason the Torah lengthened its account of these wells is that the benefit does not reach us so much from this, nor is it a great honor for Yitzchak; rather, the reason for the account is that they are a hint to the future. For 'a well of living water' alludes to the Temple, as it is written (Jeremiah 17:13): 'מקור מים חיים את ה׳'; and the first well he named <b>Esek</b>, alluding to the First Temple, concerning which our nation was wronged and they made against us many disputes and many wars until they destroyed it. And the name of the second he called <b>Sitnah</b> — a harsher name than the first — alluding to the Second Temple, concerning which it is written (Ezra 4:6): 'And in the reign of Ahasuerus, at the beginning of his reign, they wrote שטנה against the inhabitants of Judah and Jerusalem,' and all its days they were to us for a satan, until they destroyed it and we were exiled from it in a bitter exile." "<b>And you should be roused</b> regarding the name of this well: for there is found the name of the Satan, who is the prince of Esav, who informs against us continually. And the name of the third well, which will be rebuilt speedily in our days, he called <b>Rehovot.</b> And Scripture mentions 'and he moved from there' — with its well — 'and he moved,' that is, he departed from the Second Temple and was gathered to the building of the Third Temple; this is 'and he dug another well, and they did not strive over it, and he called its name Rehovot' — after what is written of the future Third Temple (Ezekiel 41:7): 'ורחבה ונסבה למעלה למעלה'; and it will be made without strife and contention, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will broaden our borders, as it is said (Deuteronomy 12:20): 'ואם ירחיב ה׳ את גבולך כאשר דבר לך' — which is spoken of the future. End quote." "<b>And the fact</b> that he mentioned the name HaShem regarding the third well, and did not do so regarding the first two — it is possible to say the reason: for the first ones were a building of flesh and blood, by means of Shelomo and Koresh; but the third will be the deed of HaShem, a heavenly building, and destruction will not rule over it. Therefore he mentioned in it the name YHWH (הש״י)." "<b>And I found</b> a Midrash supporting this, for so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (II Samuel 5:7): 'וילכד דוד את מצודת ציון היא עיר דוד' — why is it called after the name of flesh and blood? But because it was revealed and known before the King of kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, that flesh and blood build it and flesh and blood destroy it; therefore it is called after the name of flesh and blood. But in the time to come it is called after His name, blessed be He, as it is said (Isaiah 60:14): 'והלכו אליך שחוח בני מעניך והשתחוו' etc. 'וקראו לך עיר ה׳ ציון קדוש ישראל.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {3 "Verse citation normalized from Hebrew forms: עזרא ד׳:ו׳ → Ezra 4:6; שמואל ב ה׳:ז׳ → II Samuel 5:7."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:33.612850201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}